// Seasonality-aware month-end forecast — replaces [Forecast Month-End
// Cost]'s flat linear extrapolation (days elapsed ÷ days in month) with
// one that weights each remaining day by its historical day-of-week
// share of volume, using data the heatmap (file 10) already proves is
// real: a naive linear forecast assumes every remaining day looks like
// the average day so far, which is wrong if, say, weekends run at a
// fraction of weekday volume — the linear version would systematically
// over- or under-forecast depending on which weekdays are left in the
// month.
//
// REQUIRES `Day Of Week Number` and `Date Only` from
// 10-heatmap-moving-average.dax.
//
// CORRECTED (this session's own review): an earlier revision computed
// each weekday's average cost via a calculated table (`WeekdayProfile`),
// built once at model refresh from the WHOLE, unfiltered container with
// no relationship back to it. That table couldn't respond to a page
// slicer/filter (cost center, product, deployment...) — the entire point
// of the Cost-Center and Individual-User pages this kit builds — so
// `ActualSoFar` (filter-aware) and `ForecastRemaining` (always org-wide)
// ended up on different scales the moment a report page was filtered,
// making the sum meaningless for anything but the unfiltered Org page.
// It also silently zeroed a remaining day's contribution (SUMX treats a
// blank SELECTEDVALUE as 0) whenever a weekday had no historical rows
// yet in the active filter context — understating the forecast in
// exactly the low-data regime this measure should be most careful about.
//
// Fixed below by computing each weekday's average INLINE inside the
// measure, via a CALCULATE that only ADDS a weekday filter on top of
// whatever context is already active — so it inherits the same cost-
// center/product/deployment scoping ActualSoFar already has — with an
// explicit COALESCE fallback to the (also filter-aware) overall average
// when a specific weekday has no history yet.

Seasonality-Aware Forecast Month-End Cost =
VAR CurrentDate = INT(MAX('llm-usage-container'[timestamp]))
VAR MonthEnd = EOMONTH(CurrentDate, 0)
VAR RemainingDays = MonthEnd - CurrentDate
VAR ActualSoFar = [Total Cost (incl. PTU)]
VAR OverallAvgDailyCost =
    DIVIDE([Total Cost (incl. PTU)], DISTINCTCOUNT('llm-usage-container'[Date Only]))
VAR ForecastRemaining =
    IF(
        RemainingDays > 0,
        SUMX(
            GENERATESERIES(CurrentDate + 1, MonthEnd, 1),
            VAR ThisWeekdayNum = WEEKDAY([Value], 2)
            VAR ThisWeekdayAvg =
                CALCULATE(
                    DIVIDE([Total Cost (incl. PTU)], DISTINCTCOUNT('llm-usage-container'[Date Only])),
                    'llm-usage-container'[Day Of Week Number] = ThisWeekdayNum
                )
            RETURN
                COALESCE(ThisWeekdayAvg, OverallAvgDailyCost)
        ),
        0
    )
    // GENERATESERIES is only ever called inside this branch, evaluated
    // only when RemainingDays > 0 — guarantees CurrentDate+1 <= MonthEnd,
    // so the series is never called with a reversed/invalid range (e.g.
    // on the last day of the month, where naively calling it unconditionally
    // could pass start > end).
RETURN
    ActualSoFar + ForecastRemaining

Forecast Delta (Seasonality vs Linear) =
[Seasonality-Aware Forecast Month-End Cost] - [Forecast Month-End Cost]
// Put both forecasts and this delta on the same KPI row — makes the
// improvement visible instead of silently swapping one number for
// another. A consistently large delta one direction is itself useful
// information about how lumpy this organization's weekly usage pattern
// really is.
